CRITERION 2a -- CODE PATHS AS DESCRIBED: PASS
  snapshot.go:167-171 sits immediately above snapshot.go:172
  `err = filepath.Walk(s.cfg.DataDir, func(path string, info os.FileInfo, walkErr error) error {`
  Inside that closure: line 181 `if info.IsDir() { ... return nil }` (dir branch),
  line 190 `rel, err := filepath.Rel(s.cfg.DataDir, path)`, line 195
  `rel = filepath.ToSlash(rel)`, line 197 `raw, err := os.ReadFile(path)`, line 202
  `w, err := zw.Create(rel)`. So non-dir entries are read with os.ReadFile and
  archived under the walked path's own relative name -- as the comment states.
  restore.go:278-284 is the contiguous doc comment of
  `func (s *Service) pruneExtras(writer *storage.ExclusiveWriter, ...)` at line 285.
  Trace of the removal path (grep + read of internal/services/storage/storage.go):
    restore.go:320  `if err := writer.Remove(pathAbs); err != nil {`   (stale files)
    restore.go:340  `if err := writer.Remove(dir); ...`                (empty dirs)
    storage.go:788  `func (w *ExclusiveWriter) Remove(path string) error {`
    storage.go:792  `return w.s.removeLocked(path)`
    storage.go:725  `func (s *Storage) removeLocked(path string) error {`
    storage.go:731  `err := os.Remove(path)`
  writer.Remove bottoms out in os.Remove with no Lstat/Readlink/RemoveAll and no
  symlink resolution anywhere on the path -- "writer.Remove to os.Remove" is exact.

CRITERION 2b -- BEHAVIORAL SPOT-CHECK ON THIS KERNEL: PASS
  /tmp/t11/behav/main.go builds a tree with a symlinked file and a symlinked
  directory pointing outside the walked root, then walks it exactly as buildZip does.
  Output:
    walk visit rel=link.txt       IsDir()=false lstat-mode=SYMLINK(lstat)
        os.ReadFile(link.txt) -> "TARGET-BYTES-XYZ"  (archived under rel name "link.txt")
    walk visit rel=linkdir        IsDir()=false lstat-mode=SYMLINK(lstat)
        os.ReadFile(linkdir) -> ERROR: read .../data/linkdir: is a directory
    walk visit rel=real.txt       IsDir()=false lstat-mode=regular
        os.ReadFile(real.txt) -> "REAL-FILE-BYTES"
  -> symlink (file OR dir) is visited with info.IsDir()==false, i.e. a non-dir entry
     "regardless of its target"; the symlinked file's bytes returned by os.ReadFile
     are the TARGET's bytes ("TARGET-BYTES-XYZ"), keyed under the LINK's relative
     name "link.txt"; and linkdir/inner.txt is never visited -- the symlinked
     directory's contents are not walked. All three snapshot-comment claims hold.
  Part B (os.Remove on a symlink):
    link path after os.Remove: GONE (unlinked) -> lstat ...: no such file or directory
    target after os.Remove(link): readErr=<nil> content="TARGET-BYTES-XYZ"
    target lstat: exists=true size=16
  -> unlink, not a target wipe: the link is dropped, the target survives with its
     content intact. The restore-comment claim holds.

CRITERION 3 -- NO COMPLETENESS CLAIM (T6 LESSON): PASS
  Mechanical scan of the added lines:
  `grep -inE '\b(always|every|all|only|never|any|guarantee[sd]?|cannot|impossible)\b' /tmp/t11/added.txt`
    -> no matches (exit 1).
  Wording judged directly, not just grepped. snapshot.go: "filepath.Walk uses Lstat,
  so a symlink is a non-dir entry regardless of its target: a symlinked file is read
  through by os.ReadFile below and archived under the link's relative name, while a
  symlinked directory's contents are not walked here." Every clause is scoped to this
  walk ("below", "here") and is a direct consequence of Lstat, verified in 2b for both
  a file target and a directory target. restore.go: "Removal goes through writer.Remove
  to os.Remove -- unlink, not a target wipe -- so pruning a symlink drops the link at
  that path and leaves whatever it pointed at untouched." Scoped to "that path";
  no assertion about broader backup/restore behaviour, no exhaustive quantifier.

CRITERION 4 -- CROSS-REFERENCE ACCURATE, NOT AN OVERCLAIM: PASS
  Snapshot comment: "Matches the data directory's contract (see storage.atomicWrite):
  symlinks there are not honoured."
  internal/services/storage/storage.go:605-615, the doc comment of
  `func (s *Storage) atomicWrite(path string, data []byte, perm os.FileMode) error`
  (line 621), reads: "The rename below replaces whatever currently sits at path,
  symlink or not: if path is a symlink, the rename removes the link and puts a regular
  file in its place, so the file the link used to point at is left untouched ... Files
  under Storage are expected to be real files; symlinks in the data directory are not
  honoured."
  The referenced symbol exists, is spelled correctly, and the quoted contract is
  verbatim ("symlinks in the data directory are not honoured"). The cross-reference
  asserts nothing storage.go does not already assert.

